1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,940

2
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:08,480
Let's get started for today.

3
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,962
Let's start with a recap of
what we covered last time

4
00:00:10,962 --> 00:00:12,920
because we're going to
be building very heavily

5
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:16,120
on Wednesday's concepts.

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,480
So we talked about
online reinforcement

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:21,600
learning where we collect a
batch of data with some policy

8
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,840
by running that policy
in the environment,

9
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:28,440
and then we try to use that
data to improve our policy,

10
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:33,560
and then we repeat this
process in order to continue

11
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,303
to improve from practice.

12
00:00:36,303 --> 00:00:38,720
We talked about an online
reinforcement learning algorithm

13
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,640
called policy gradients.

14
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:44,040
And the update for our
policy looks something

15
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:49,060
like this, where we first
take samples from our policy.

16
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:51,600
This will give us N
different trajectories

17
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:53,880
sampled from our policy.

18
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:57,460
We will evaluate the log
likelihood of the policy.

19
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,980
And so this pi here is
representing a distribution

20
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:02,160
over actions given states.

21
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:03,900
And so this is looking
like the gradient

22
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:09,380
of the log probability of
the actions under the policy.

23
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:13,380
And this log likelihood
is weighted by the reward

24
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,240
that the agent got.

25
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,940
And so if you have
higher rewards

26
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:20,580
as an outcome of your
actions in the future,

27
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:23,020
then you're going to be
encouraging the policy

28
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:26,620
to increase the likelihood
of those actions.

29
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:30,660
And then lastly, we
introduced a baseline,

30
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:33,100
which we could define to
be the average reward, such

31
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,220
that we're increasing the
likelihood on futures that

32
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:39,020
were above average
reward and decreasing

33
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:43,740
the likelihood on actions
that led to futures

34
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:47,180
that were below average reward.

35
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:50,500
So you can think of this
as encouraging the policy

36
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:52,360
to do more of the
above average stuff,

37
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,100
less of the below
average stuff, and then

38
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:59,290
repeating that process
to get better and better.

39
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:00,690
Cool.

40
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:04,530
We also talked about how this
algorithm is fully on-policy.

41
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:08,370
So in order to evaluate
this, we actually

42
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:12,075
need to have samples from pi
theta from our current policy.

43
00:02:12,075 --> 00:02:13,450
And this meant
that we could only

44
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:17,050
take one gradient step per batch
of data, which is disappointing

45
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:19,610
because then we'd have
to collect ton of data

46
00:02:19,610 --> 00:02:21,370
in order to improve.

47
00:02:21,370 --> 00:02:23,690
This brings up this
notion of having on-policy

48
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:26,610
versus off-policy algorithms
that can reuse data

49
00:02:26,610 --> 00:02:29,050
from other past policies.

50
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:34,690
And we started to look at ways
to make an algorithm off-policy.

51
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:37,730
And in particular, we talked
about using importance weights,

52
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:41,930
where if we weight the
objective by the probability

53
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:45,170
of the actions of the
new policy divided

54
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:48,130
by the probability of the
actions under the old policy,

55
00:02:48,130 --> 00:02:52,090
this is a way to get a
estimate of the gradient

56
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:53,660
for the new policy.

57
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:56,370

58
00:02:56,370 --> 00:02:59,270
I should also mention
importance weights.

59
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:02,510
The math behind it is
very simple and very nice.

60
00:03:02,510 --> 00:03:05,990
But it's important to remember
that this isn't a silver bullet.

61
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:09,430
This approach is only
suitable when the policies are

62
00:03:09,430 --> 00:03:11,110
very similar to one another.

63
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:12,930
And if the policies
are very different,

64
00:03:12,930 --> 00:03:16,390
then this ratio will get
very large or very small,

65
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:19,520
and you'll also just have a
worse estimate of your gradient.

66
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:22,390

67
00:03:22,390 --> 00:03:24,310
So today we're
going to be talking

68
00:03:24,310 --> 00:03:27,830
about a form of algorithms
called actor-critic methods that

69
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:29,590
build on policy gradients.

70
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:32,215

71
00:03:32,215 --> 00:03:33,590
As part of this,
we'll be talking

72
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:35,830
about how to estimate
the value of a policy,

73
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:38,830
and then we'll introduce
an off-policy variant

74
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:40,950
of the algorithm.

75
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:44,710
And these actor-critic
methods are the basis

76
00:03:44,710 --> 00:03:47,270
for algorithms
like PPO, which are

77
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:50,150
used to train language
models, and that

78
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:57,130
are used to train humanoids
and simulation and so forth.

79
00:03:57,130 --> 00:04:00,702
So very, very useful
RL algorithms.

80
00:04:00,702 --> 00:04:02,410
And then the key
learning goals for today

81
00:04:02,410 --> 00:04:05,330
is how to estimate how
good a state and action is

82
00:04:05,330 --> 00:04:06,850
for a policy.

83
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:09,410
This is called
policy evaluation.

84
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:12,770
It's a critical aspect
of actor-critic methods

85
00:04:12,770 --> 00:04:14,690
but also useful more broadly.

86
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:16,290
And then also talk
about how to use

87
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:18,850
those estimates to form a
better reinforcement learning

88
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:19,550
algorithm.

89
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:24,370

90
00:04:24,370 --> 00:04:25,650
So let's get started.

91
00:04:25,650 --> 00:04:27,590
And to talk about
these algorithms,

92
00:04:27,590 --> 00:04:32,370
we need to revisit some very
useful objects that we talked

93
00:04:32,370 --> 00:04:35,010
about in the first lecture,
and specifically, this

94
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:36,970
is value functions.

95
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:41,090
And so a value function
is representing

96
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:45,490
how good a state
is if you're going

97
00:04:45,490 --> 00:04:48,610
to follow a policy
starting from that state.

98
00:04:48,610 --> 00:04:51,290
And you can think
of it as the sum

99
00:04:51,290 --> 00:04:55,830
of rewards used to
writing gradients

100
00:04:55,830 --> 00:04:57,690
starting from some state.

101
00:04:57,690 --> 00:05:02,102
So we'll write
this as V pi of S.

102
00:05:02,102 --> 00:05:04,310
This means that we're going
to be starting at state S

103
00:05:04,310 --> 00:05:06,070
and then running pi.

104
00:05:06,070 --> 00:05:14,290
And for a given state, this
is equal to the sum of rewards

105
00:05:14,290 --> 00:05:22,670
if we were to start
at state t and go

106
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:24,750
to the end of the trajectory.

107
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:29,590
And here's the sum of rewards.

108
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:33,870
Now, this sum of
rewards is going

109
00:05:33,870 --> 00:05:37,510
to differ depending on
what state we start at

110
00:05:37,510 --> 00:05:39,730
and depending on what
policy that we're running.

111
00:05:39,730 --> 00:05:49,590
And so this is going to be an
expectation under our policy.

112
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:52,937
Actually maybe it's easier
to write this as expectation

113
00:05:52,937 --> 00:05:53,520
on the inside.

114
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:59,660
So this is an expectation
over the states St prime

115
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:05,940
and actions at prime that will
be visited under our policy

116
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:14,060
and conditioned on the fact
that we're starting at state St.

117
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:17,700
So we can think about the
value function as this.

118
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,900
Just in terms of math, what you
can think about this as is--

119
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:24,420
or just in terms of conceptually
or in terms of intuition,

120
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:26,580
if you have some
trajectory, and you're

121
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:30,500
thinking about the value of this
state, you can think of it as--

122
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,140
if we were to collect a lot
of different trajectories

123
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:36,740
starting from that state
from our policy, and then

124
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:41,880
we just summed up the rewards
from this time step to the end,

125
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:43,980
and then averaged over
these trajectories,

126
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:47,140
that would define the
value of this state

127
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:51,640
or how good the state
is for the policy.

128
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:55,200
Another useful quantity
that will be making use of

129
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:56,448
is called the Q function.

130
00:06:56,448 --> 00:06:58,740
And this is slightly different
from the value function,

131
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:00,600
but very closely related.

132
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:03,520
And the difference is that
instead of just thinking

133
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:05,195
about the value
of a given state,

134
00:07:05,195 --> 00:07:07,320
we're going to be thinking
about a value of a given

135
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,560
state and action.

136
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:15,640
And what this is referring to
is instead of starting at S

137
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:18,320
and then running pi from
there, if instead we

138
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:21,500
were to start at S, take
a particular action a,

139
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:27,600
and then run the policy, what
is our expected sum of rewards?

140
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,760
And so then you can
think of this as--

141
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:34,720
instead of a picture
like this, say

142
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,120
we had some State s
that we considered--

143
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:40,915
first we're going to
take some given action.

144
00:07:40,915 --> 00:07:43,040
This may or may not have
been what the policy would

145
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:46,160
have been likely to choose.

146
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,326
This action could actually
also lead to different states

147
00:07:49,326 --> 00:07:54,500
because the environment
is stochastic,

148
00:07:54,500 --> 00:08:03,700
and then it's the sum of
these rewards on average.

149
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:06,380
So this is what V looks like.

150
00:08:06,380 --> 00:08:10,250
This is what Q looks
like for a given action.

151
00:08:10,250 --> 00:08:13,980

152
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:16,120
So here are some
pictures on the slide.

153
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:19,060
So if we start from
state s, run our policy,

154
00:08:19,060 --> 00:08:22,780
we compute the expected
sum of the rewards

155
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:25,220
along these
trajectories, then that

156
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:28,340
gets us the value of a state.

157
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:32,740
And then if instead we take
action a first and then run pi,

158
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:36,900
then we get the Q values, and
it's just the sum of the rewards

159
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:38,600
if we had started with action a.

160
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:42,380

161
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:46,220
Now, one thing that's useful
to think about in terms

162
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:47,820
of how these two
relate to each other

163
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:51,840
is you can think of the
value of a state as--

164
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,840
if you take an expectation
over the actions

165
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:55,480
that the policy
would have taken,

166
00:08:55,480 --> 00:08:59,080
you can think of it as the
average or the expectation

167
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:01,400
of the Q values
over all the actions

168
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:04,920
that the policy
would have taken.

169
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:09,240
And then one last object
that we'll use today

170
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:13,440
is thinking about how good
is a particular action

171
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:18,040
relative to other actions
that we might take.

172
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:22,520
And for this, we
can think about,

173
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:28,960
if you consider the Q value
minus the value function, that's

174
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:31,040
going to tell you, how
good is it to take action

175
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:35,000
a versus following the
policy at a given state.

176
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,520
And this is what
is the advantage

177
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:42,717
of taking one action versus
following the policy.

178
00:09:42,717 --> 00:09:43,300
Good question.

179
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:50,010
So the question was, is there a
normalization between V and Q?

180
00:09:50,010 --> 00:09:52,550

181
00:09:52,550 --> 00:09:55,910
You can think of an
expectation as an average.

182
00:09:55,910 --> 00:09:58,070
And so in that
sense, there is going

183
00:09:58,070 --> 00:10:00,990
to be a normalization
because if you were to,

184
00:10:00,990 --> 00:10:04,110
for example, try to estimate
this expectation with samples,

185
00:10:04,110 --> 00:10:06,370
you would take some
number of samples.

186
00:10:06,370 --> 00:10:08,830
If you only use these two
samples to estimate Q,

187
00:10:08,830 --> 00:10:10,810
then you would divide
by 2, versus here,

188
00:10:10,810 --> 00:10:13,910
if you're using five samples,
you would divide by 5.

189
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:16,310
Is there a way that you
can get from this equation

190
00:10:16,310 --> 00:10:18,350
to the useful
relation on the slide?

191
00:10:18,350 --> 00:10:20,810
Is that what you're asking?

192
00:10:20,810 --> 00:10:21,310
Yeah.

193
00:10:21,310 --> 00:10:28,790

194
00:10:28,790 --> 00:10:31,170
We can write out first
what Q pi looks like.

195
00:10:31,170 --> 00:10:40,410
So Q pi I of a given
state and action.

196
00:10:40,410 --> 00:10:44,710
We're going to condition
on St, at because that's

197
00:10:44,710 --> 00:10:45,870
where we're starting.

198
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:49,430
And then this is the expected
sum of rewards if we--

199
00:10:49,430 --> 00:10:52,530

200
00:10:52,530 --> 00:10:57,760
So a sum over t
prime equals t to T.

201
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:00,010
And then we're going to have
an expectation of running

202
00:11:00,010 --> 00:11:07,980
the policy here, and
then a sum of rewards.

203
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:12,690

204
00:11:12,690 --> 00:11:24,225
And then in terms of seeing how
to get between these two things,

205
00:11:24,225 --> 00:11:27,290
you can think of this
as a sum of rewards

206
00:11:27,290 --> 00:11:34,890
under at future actions
sampled from pi,

207
00:11:34,890 --> 00:11:38,550
whereas this one at is
not sampled from pi,

208
00:11:38,550 --> 00:11:40,810
but all future actions
are sampled from pi.

209
00:11:40,810 --> 00:11:45,270
In this case, we're
conditioning on the action

210
00:11:45,270 --> 00:11:46,810
that's provided as input.

211
00:11:46,810 --> 00:11:49,330
And in this case, we're
sampling from the policy.

212
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:56,110
And so if instead you sample
the action from the policy

213
00:11:56,110 --> 00:12:05,190
and then feed that in to here,
then you'll get this right here.

214
00:12:05,190 --> 00:12:08,670
So to help you start thinking
about some of these quantities,

215
00:12:08,670 --> 00:12:10,550
let's look at an example.

216
00:12:10,550 --> 00:12:15,110
And so say that you are
this person right here.

217
00:12:15,110 --> 00:12:19,150
You really want to learn
how to play the drums.

218
00:12:19,150 --> 00:12:26,510
And your reward is 1 if you can
play the music in a month, and 0

219
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:27,210
otherwise.

220
00:12:27,210 --> 00:12:29,870
So capital T is
a month from now.

221
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:33,630
And you have three
different possible actions

222
00:12:33,630 --> 00:12:34,950
in your action space.

223
00:12:34,950 --> 00:12:37,510
One action is to
sit on the beach.

224
00:12:37,510 --> 00:12:40,950
Another action is to watch TV.

225
00:12:40,950 --> 00:12:45,740
And another action is to
practice playing the drums.

226
00:12:45,740 --> 00:12:48,540
And say that your current
policy right now is just

227
00:12:48,540 --> 00:12:54,140
to sit on the beach, and
you do that for all states.

228
00:12:54,140 --> 00:12:56,660
Now, my question for
you to think about

229
00:12:56,660 --> 00:13:02,680
is, what are each of these
quantities for the action of a1,

230
00:13:02,680 --> 00:13:05,640
for the action of a2,
and for the action of a3.

231
00:13:05,640 --> 00:13:09,210
And so specifically you
want to think about,

232
00:13:09,210 --> 00:13:11,780
what is the value function
of your current state

233
00:13:11,780 --> 00:13:15,100
for this policy?

234
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:18,420
What is your Q
function if you were

235
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:22,380
to follow each of these
actions once and then follow

236
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:23,660
this policy?

237
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:29,020
And then what is your advantage
function for the current state

238
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:33,060
that you're in and for
each of these actions?

239
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:36,500
And you can assume that in the
current state that you're in,

240
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:40,020
you do not know how
to play the drums.

241
00:13:40,020 --> 00:13:42,733
What was described as
for the value function,

242
00:13:42,733 --> 00:13:44,400
for this particular
policy that's always

243
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:47,000
sitting on the
beach, the value is 0

244
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:51,100
because the person would
not learn to play the drums,

245
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:53,680
so that's the sum of expected
rewards for this policy,

246
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:55,640
for V pi.

247
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:59,360
For the Q function, if you sit
on the beach at the current time

248
00:13:59,360 --> 00:14:04,050
step or watch TV, the Q function
will be 0 for those actions.

249
00:14:04,050 --> 00:14:06,920

250
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:10,480
The Q function for playing the
drums at the current time step

251
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:15,140
would be 1 if that amount
of practicing is sufficient.

252
00:14:15,140 --> 00:14:17,420
It's also possible that it
would be a bit lower than 1

253
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:20,720
if you, for example,
practice for an hour

254
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:23,380
and then sit on the beach by
following the policy afterwards,

255
00:14:23,380 --> 00:14:29,160
it depends a little bit on
how long a given time step is.

256
00:14:29,160 --> 00:14:33,020
And then the advantage function
is going to be Q minus V,

257
00:14:33,020 --> 00:14:35,520
and so it's going to
be a positive number

258
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:37,640
for the action of
practicing, and it'll

259
00:14:37,640 --> 00:14:41,380
be 0 for the other two actions.

260
00:14:41,380 --> 00:14:44,260
So the question is,
if practicing is not

261
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:47,000
enough to actually
learn, would it be 1?

262
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:48,240
Would it be between 0 and 1?

263
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:50,540
Would it be 0?

264
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:53,620
It basically actually
represents the probability

265
00:14:53,620 --> 00:14:56,380
in which you would be able to
play this, given that you had

266
00:14:56,380 --> 00:14:57,820
practiced for that
amount of time,

267
00:14:57,820 --> 00:15:01,800
and that probability
it could be 0.

268
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:05,763
Maybe this is really, really
hard after just practicing once.

269
00:15:05,763 --> 00:15:07,180
It could also be
maybe for someone

270
00:15:07,180 --> 00:15:08,720
who's extremely talented.

271
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:10,260
They only need to practice once.

272
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:12,135
And so that could be a
bit higher for someone

273
00:15:12,135 --> 00:15:14,140
that's a bit more talented.

274
00:15:14,140 --> 00:15:17,860
It depends on various
factors like that.

275
00:15:17,860 --> 00:15:20,860
Along those lines, if you
think about the value function,

276
00:15:20,860 --> 00:15:24,180
if you're playing a game
of chess, for example,

277
00:15:24,180 --> 00:15:26,260
the value function
is the probability

278
00:15:26,260 --> 00:15:31,700
that you're going to
win for a given person

279
00:15:31,700 --> 00:15:33,000
and for a given game state.

280
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,860
So it's evaluating
how good is the state

281
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:38,118
for a particular person.

282
00:15:38,118 --> 00:15:39,660
That's another way
to think about it.

283
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:43,860

284
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:47,320
So those are the
objects that we're going

285
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:49,480
to be using quite a bit today.

286
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:52,200
Now let's go back
to policy gradients

287
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:54,080
and see how we can
use value functions

288
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:57,120
to make this algorithm better.

289
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:59,360
So we talked about
a couple examples

290
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:04,280
on Wednesday of some scenarios
that we're dissatisfying

291
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:06,080
about policy gradients.

292
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:10,160
And the first was that if we
are trying to learn how to walk

293
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:12,560
and we have a policy that
takes a small step forwards

294
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:16,400
and then falls backwards,
we will push down

295
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:18,880
the likelihood of
taking a step forward

296
00:16:18,880 --> 00:16:20,960
because we don't
necessarily know that might

297
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:23,440
have led it to fall backwards.

298
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:26,120
Now, if we actually
had some understanding

299
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:28,400
that taking a small step
forward was making progress,

300
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:31,080
was actually good,
was advantageous,

301
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:33,560
then we might be able
to actually increase

302
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:35,280
the likelihood of
that, and so this

303
00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:37,270
is starting to hint at
some of the intuition

304
00:16:37,270 --> 00:16:40,870
behind actor-critic methods.

305
00:16:40,870 --> 00:16:43,510
And likewise, we talked
about this example

306
00:16:43,510 --> 00:16:45,390
of trying to fold a jacket.

307
00:16:45,390 --> 00:16:49,990
And we found that if
you have a sparse reward

308
00:16:49,990 --> 00:16:52,670
and you only do part of the
task by folding only the sleeves

309
00:16:52,670 --> 00:16:55,470
or flattening the jacket
but not folding it,

310
00:16:55,470 --> 00:16:58,910
you aren't really utilizing
the trajectories that

311
00:16:58,910 --> 00:17:01,910
are getting zero reward.

312
00:17:01,910 --> 00:17:06,150
And similar to this
case, if we instead

313
00:17:06,150 --> 00:17:08,750
understood that these actions
are advantageous for making

314
00:17:08,750 --> 00:17:10,750
progress on the task,
then we might actually

315
00:17:10,750 --> 00:17:13,470
be able to figure out how
to use these trajectories

316
00:17:13,470 --> 00:17:15,349
in a positive way.

317
00:17:15,349 --> 00:17:18,270
And so to summarize
here, policy gradients

318
00:17:18,270 --> 00:17:20,630
don't always make an
efficient use of data

319
00:17:20,630 --> 00:17:22,030
that we've collected.

320
00:17:22,030 --> 00:17:25,974
And if we can try to figure out
what actions are advantageous

321
00:17:25,974 --> 00:17:27,349
versus not
advantageous, we might

322
00:17:27,349 --> 00:17:30,870
be able to make better
use of that data.

323
00:17:30,870 --> 00:17:36,230
So the question was, if a step
forward is helpful or not,

324
00:17:36,230 --> 00:17:37,900
is that going to be
negative or neutral?

325
00:17:37,900 --> 00:17:41,250

326
00:17:41,250 --> 00:17:46,085
In case of this policy gradient
algorithm, the trajectory

327
00:17:46,085 --> 00:17:48,210
that we considered here in
the context of the batch

328
00:17:48,210 --> 00:17:52,130
of trajectories, net
negative was below average

329
00:17:52,130 --> 00:17:55,230
because the reward to
go is quite negative.

330
00:17:55,230 --> 00:18:01,330
It's actually making backwards
progress in this trajectory.

331
00:18:01,330 --> 00:18:03,410
And so it actually is
going to be pushing down

332
00:18:03,410 --> 00:18:05,970
the likelihood of
taking a step forward

333
00:18:05,970 --> 00:18:09,810
because the future
reward to go is negative.

334
00:18:09,810 --> 00:18:12,370
And what we'll
talk about today is

335
00:18:12,370 --> 00:18:14,490
algorithms that in
principle could actually

336
00:18:14,490 --> 00:18:16,850
increase the likelihood
for taking the step forward

337
00:18:16,850 --> 00:18:19,150
and decrease the likelihood
for falling backwards.

338
00:18:19,150 --> 00:18:23,250

339
00:18:23,250 --> 00:18:26,570
So how do we do that?

340
00:18:26,570 --> 00:18:30,590
A lot of what we'll focus on
today is this piece right here.

341
00:18:30,590 --> 00:18:32,507
This is what's
actually telling us

342
00:18:32,507 --> 00:18:34,590
that we should push down
the likelihood if we have

343
00:18:34,590 --> 00:18:35,882
negative rewards in the future.

344
00:18:35,882 --> 00:18:38,710

345
00:18:38,710 --> 00:18:41,550
And this quantity, it's
estimating the future

346
00:18:41,550 --> 00:18:47,510
rewards if we take
action a in state s.

347
00:18:47,510 --> 00:18:50,590
And so we can
think about it as--

348
00:18:50,590 --> 00:18:54,590
in this picture right
here, it looks a lot like,

349
00:18:54,590 --> 00:18:58,430
what is the sum of rewards
if we started right

350
00:18:58,430 --> 00:19:02,950
here with state s and action a.

351
00:19:02,950 --> 00:19:06,788
And then if we think
about this picture,

352
00:19:06,788 --> 00:19:08,830
we were looking at this
quantity in terms of what

353
00:19:08,830 --> 00:19:10,870
we observed in trajectories.

354
00:19:10,870 --> 00:19:15,250
But what would be an even
better version of this quantity

355
00:19:15,250 --> 00:19:18,350
would be to actually
use the Q function.

356
00:19:18,350 --> 00:19:21,230
And if we could replace
this with the Q function,

357
00:19:21,230 --> 00:19:24,912
then we would be able to get
a much better estimate of

358
00:19:24,912 --> 00:19:26,870
whether or not we should
push up the likelihood

359
00:19:26,870 --> 00:19:29,070
or push down the likelihood.

360
00:19:29,070 --> 00:19:31,630
And so looking at the
slides right now, what we do

361
00:19:31,630 --> 00:19:34,700
is, for a single
trajectory, we sum together

362
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:38,140
the rewards that we saw, which
was, in the case of the example

363
00:19:38,140 --> 00:19:39,980
before, the rewards
were negative

364
00:19:39,980 --> 00:19:43,820
because we took a small step
forward and then fell backwards.

365
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:48,580
But if instead what we could
do is actually run the policy

366
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:54,460
many, many times and get a
Q function for the state,

367
00:19:54,460 --> 00:19:56,300
that would be the
true expected rewards

368
00:19:56,300 --> 00:19:59,700
to go under the
expectation of our policy,

369
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:01,640
and this would be way better.

370
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:06,740
We'd be able to get a much
better sense for how good it

371
00:20:06,740 --> 00:20:09,160
was to take that
action in that state.

372
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:12,900

373
00:20:12,900 --> 00:20:17,180
So essentially the
form of the gradient

374
00:20:17,180 --> 00:20:19,323
would be able to get
a much better gradient

375
00:20:19,323 --> 00:20:20,740
if the form of the
gradient looked

376
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:24,700
like this, where we increase
the likelihood of the actions

377
00:20:24,700 --> 00:20:26,447
for things that
have high Q values,

378
00:20:26,447 --> 00:20:28,780
and we decrease the likelihood
of the actions for things

379
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:30,630
that have low Q values.

380
00:20:30,630 --> 00:20:33,760

381
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:34,990
So that's a first step.

382
00:20:34,990 --> 00:20:40,280

383
00:20:40,280 --> 00:20:42,840
We also talked about baselines
in the previous lecture

384
00:20:42,840 --> 00:20:46,840
where the baseline was
the average reward.

385
00:20:46,840 --> 00:20:49,240
Should we use a baseline here?

386
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:52,480
So before the baselines
were the average reward.

387
00:20:52,480 --> 00:20:55,400
And so in this case, if we
were to introduce baselines,

388
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:58,880
we could think about
what we might use.

389
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:03,460
And if we use the same
thing that we used before,

390
00:21:03,460 --> 00:21:08,560
we could use the average
Q value, in this case.

391
00:21:08,560 --> 00:21:14,200
So we could use an average--

392
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:23,400
maybe we sample some Si and
ai, and we average over those.

393
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:27,200
And what we had talked
about a few slides ago

394
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:32,400
is this is actually looking a
lot like the value function.

395
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:35,780
So we talked about the
value of a given state

396
00:21:35,780 --> 00:21:41,900
is an expectation
over actions sampled

397
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:47,380
from the policy for that
given state of the Q

398
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:53,020
function for those actions.

399
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:54,860
This is V pi.

400
00:21:54,860 --> 00:21:57,820
And so a natural
baseline would actually

401
00:21:57,820 --> 00:22:00,700
be to use the value function.

402
00:22:00,700 --> 00:22:05,820
And so instead of
having this be Q values,

403
00:22:05,820 --> 00:22:10,100
we could actually use Q
minus V. And Q minus V

404
00:22:10,100 --> 00:22:13,380
is what the advantage
function is.

405
00:22:13,380 --> 00:22:16,060
And so putting this
together, we would

406
00:22:16,060 --> 00:22:20,780
be increasing the
likelihood for actions

407
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:23,940
that have a high
advantage and decreasing

408
00:22:23,940 --> 00:22:26,980
the likelihood for actions
that have a low advantage.

409
00:22:26,980 --> 00:22:31,280
And so then if you think back
to the example of learning

410
00:22:31,280 --> 00:22:36,840
to practice playing the drums,
the action of practicing

411
00:22:36,840 --> 00:22:40,790
had a high advantage relative
to the current policy.

412
00:22:40,790 --> 00:22:43,040
And so then we would choose
to increase the likelihood

413
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:47,573
for practicing and
decrease the likelihood

414
00:22:47,573 --> 00:22:48,490
for the other actions.

415
00:22:48,490 --> 00:22:52,200

416
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:53,343
Cool.

417
00:22:53,343 --> 00:22:54,760
Another way to
think about this is

418
00:22:54,760 --> 00:22:57,560
that better estimates of
this advantage function

419
00:22:57,560 --> 00:22:59,840
will lead to less
noisy gradient.

420
00:22:59,840 --> 00:23:02,380
And if we knew the true
advantage function,

421
00:23:02,380 --> 00:23:06,165
then we would be able to get
a very, very good gradient.

422
00:23:06,165 --> 00:23:08,540
Unfortunately, we don't know
the true advantage function,

423
00:23:08,540 --> 00:23:12,160
and so the next question
that we'll talk about

424
00:23:12,160 --> 00:23:15,400
over the next several slides
is how can we estimate

425
00:23:15,400 --> 00:23:17,920
the advantage function.

426
00:23:17,920 --> 00:23:22,480
So the question was, how
is this different from how

427
00:23:22,480 --> 00:23:25,860
we're sampling in the
original policy gradient?

428
00:23:25,860 --> 00:23:31,950
And so what we're using before
is we would take of one sample,

429
00:23:31,950 --> 00:23:38,710
and then for a given state
and action in that trajectory,

430
00:23:38,710 --> 00:23:42,650
we would sum the rewards just
for that individual thing.

431
00:23:42,650 --> 00:23:45,928

432
00:23:45,928 --> 00:23:48,470
And this is going to be a very
noisy estimate because we just

433
00:23:48,470 --> 00:23:49,512
have one trajectory here.

434
00:23:49,512 --> 00:23:52,335
We just have one data point
to estimate these rewards.

435
00:23:52,335 --> 00:23:53,710
And now what we're
saying here is

436
00:23:53,710 --> 00:23:56,070
that we're going to try
to get a sense for what

437
00:23:56,070 --> 00:23:58,430
the advantage of this action is.

438
00:23:58,430 --> 00:24:01,550
Now, one way we'll
actually start

439
00:24:01,550 --> 00:24:05,572
to talk about how we
do that in a minute.

440
00:24:05,572 --> 00:24:07,030
But if you want to
think about what

441
00:24:07,030 --> 00:24:09,230
is the true advantage
of this action,

442
00:24:09,230 --> 00:24:15,150
conceptually, if you were to
take this state and action

443
00:24:15,150 --> 00:24:18,510
and then roll out the policy
an infinite number of times

444
00:24:18,510 --> 00:24:20,590
for this action
versus for following

445
00:24:20,590 --> 00:24:22,710
the policy in other
scenarios, that's

446
00:24:22,710 --> 00:24:25,690
what would give
us the advantage.

447
00:24:25,690 --> 00:24:28,710
So the question is,
in policy gradient

448
00:24:28,710 --> 00:24:32,010
we got to where we
were by optimizing

449
00:24:32,010 --> 00:24:34,490
the expected sum of rewards.

450
00:24:34,490 --> 00:24:36,930
Does this also optimize
some objective?

451
00:24:36,930 --> 00:24:39,552
So this is also optimizing
the expected sum of rewards,

452
00:24:39,552 --> 00:24:41,760
and it's actually optimizing
the same exact function.

453
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:45,210

454
00:24:45,210 --> 00:24:47,150
We haven't talked
about how to get A yet.

455
00:24:47,150 --> 00:24:51,730
But if you had a perfect
A, this would be giving you

456
00:24:51,730 --> 00:24:58,890
the correct gradient for that
objective, the sampling here

457
00:24:58,890 --> 00:25:02,210
being used to sample
states and actions.

458
00:25:02,210 --> 00:25:05,890
Is the baseline and average
over the current or an average

459
00:25:05,890 --> 00:25:07,030
over all trajectories?

460
00:25:07,030 --> 00:25:12,090

461
00:25:12,090 --> 00:25:15,930
So if we set it to be
the value function V,

462
00:25:15,930 --> 00:25:19,453
that would be an infinite
number of samples.

463
00:25:19,453 --> 00:25:21,370
We haven't yet talked
about how we're actually

464
00:25:21,370 --> 00:25:25,270
going to estimate a or
estimate Q versus V,

465
00:25:25,270 --> 00:25:27,910
so we'll talk about
that in a minute.

466
00:25:27,910 --> 00:25:31,190
But yeah, for V,
conceptually, that

467
00:25:31,190 --> 00:25:33,110
would be over an
infinite number.

468
00:25:33,110 --> 00:25:36,270
But I'm talking about, this
is the true value so far.

469
00:25:36,270 --> 00:25:39,950
We haven't talked about how
to estimate the value yet.

470
00:25:39,950 --> 00:25:42,790
So the question is,
is this unbiased?

471
00:25:42,790 --> 00:25:44,270
It isn't biased.

472
00:25:44,270 --> 00:25:45,550
You can go through the math.

473
00:25:45,550 --> 00:25:48,590
I won't go through that
today for the sake of time.

474
00:25:48,590 --> 00:25:51,070
But yeah, this is an unbiased
estimate of the gradient

475
00:25:51,070 --> 00:25:53,870
if you have the true advantage.

476
00:25:53,870 --> 00:25:57,790
Now, we will see things
that are biased later today.

477
00:25:57,790 --> 00:26:01,910
So let's actually talk
about how do we use this.

478
00:26:01,910 --> 00:26:04,550
And actually just
as a quick overview

479
00:26:04,550 --> 00:26:09,750
of what the algorithm will look
like for this form of algorithm.

480
00:26:09,750 --> 00:26:12,030
Before we had this loop
between running the policy

481
00:26:12,030 --> 00:26:14,630
and improving the
policy, now we're

482
00:26:14,630 --> 00:26:17,590
going to have an
additional step where

483
00:26:17,590 --> 00:26:19,990
we're going to fit a model
that's going to estimate

484
00:26:19,990 --> 00:26:20,930
the expected return.

485
00:26:20,930 --> 00:26:22,305
And essentially
it's going to try

486
00:26:22,305 --> 00:26:26,530
to estimate this a right here.

487
00:26:26,530 --> 00:26:28,950
And so our new algorithm will
have a few different steps.

488
00:26:28,950 --> 00:26:30,230
One is to run the policy.

489
00:26:30,230 --> 00:26:34,170
One is to try to
estimate values.

490
00:26:34,170 --> 00:26:36,010
And then the last
will be then to use

491
00:26:36,010 --> 00:26:39,050
that estimate and the
data that was collected

492
00:26:39,050 --> 00:26:42,410
to improve the policy.

493
00:26:42,410 --> 00:26:45,490
And so this step will involve
estimating either V pi Q

494
00:26:45,490 --> 00:26:47,290
pi or A pi.

495
00:26:47,290 --> 00:26:50,930
And this step will
be similar to before

496
00:26:50,930 --> 00:26:54,270
except it's going to use
these estimates right here.

497
00:26:54,270 --> 00:26:57,570

498
00:26:57,570 --> 00:27:03,170
So how do we actually
estimate these values?

499
00:27:03,170 --> 00:27:06,490
So we want to estimate
this quantity right here.

500
00:27:06,490 --> 00:27:14,050
And to start out, we
need to think about how

501
00:27:14,050 --> 00:27:16,930
we want to get to A pi.

502
00:27:16,930 --> 00:27:20,842
And we could try to
directly fit A pi,

503
00:27:20,842 --> 00:27:22,800
although it's actually
a little bit challenging

504
00:27:22,800 --> 00:27:26,960
to get direct supervision
for the advantages.

505
00:27:26,960 --> 00:27:30,620
And so we're actually
going to try to fit V pi.

506
00:27:30,620 --> 00:27:34,040
And the way that we're
going to get there is--

507
00:27:34,040 --> 00:27:36,680

508
00:27:36,680 --> 00:27:38,180
let's see, where
do we want to go?

509
00:27:38,180 --> 00:27:41,720

510
00:27:41,720 --> 00:27:55,000
We know that A pi of
St, at is equal to the Q

511
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:02,000
value minus the value.

512
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:06,820
And so one option is we could
try to estimate these two,

513
00:28:06,820 --> 00:28:09,800
although then we'd still
have to estimate two objects.

514
00:28:09,800 --> 00:28:14,000
And there's actually a way to
turn this into an equation that

515
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,920
actually only depends on V pi.

516
00:28:16,920 --> 00:28:20,380
And that would be nice because
then we could just only fit V pi

517
00:28:20,380 --> 00:28:25,500
and then derive A pi from
there to get the gradient.

518
00:28:25,500 --> 00:28:32,420
And so to do this, we can
think about Q pi right here.

519
00:28:32,420 --> 00:28:40,960
And this Q function, this is the
value of starting at S taking a

520
00:28:40,960 --> 00:28:42,860
and then summing the rewards.

521
00:28:42,860 --> 00:28:50,060
And so you can think of it as
the reward at the current time

522
00:28:50,060 --> 00:28:53,740
step that you got
plus the rewards that

523
00:28:53,740 --> 00:28:55,740
would come after that.

524
00:28:55,740 --> 00:28:59,700
And in terms of the rewards
that would come after that,

525
00:28:59,700 --> 00:29:03,980
you'll have some next
state, St plus 1.

526
00:29:03,980 --> 00:29:05,370
And so you can think of it as--

527
00:29:05,370 --> 00:29:08,420

528
00:29:08,420 --> 00:29:11,302
there's some next state.

529
00:29:11,302 --> 00:29:13,260
This next state could be
a few different things

530
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:15,360
depending on your environment.

531
00:29:15,360 --> 00:29:21,640
So this would be sampled from
the dynamics of the environment.

532
00:29:21,640 --> 00:29:28,520
And then after this
state, it's essentially

533
00:29:28,520 --> 00:29:32,160
going to be the sum of
rewards starting at St plus 1

534
00:29:32,160 --> 00:29:33,340
and following your policy.

535
00:29:33,340 --> 00:29:37,600

536
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:39,260
Sorry, this should be St plus 1.

537
00:29:39,260 --> 00:29:42,720
The sum of rewards
starting at St plus 1,

538
00:29:42,720 --> 00:29:46,760
that's equal to the
value of St plus 1.

539
00:29:46,760 --> 00:29:56,040
So we can write this Q value as
the reward at the current time

540
00:29:56,040 --> 00:30:02,000
step plus the value at
the time step after that.

541
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:04,880
Now, we can't sample
from these dynamics.

542
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:08,080
But when we ran our
policy to collect data,

543
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:12,320
we did actually see one
outcome, one St plus 1.

544
00:30:12,320 --> 00:30:17,510
And so we can approximate
this as the reward

545
00:30:17,510 --> 00:30:25,350
at the current time step plus
the value at the next time step.

546
00:30:25,350 --> 00:30:27,410
And then if we want to
estimate the advantage,

547
00:30:27,410 --> 00:30:31,070
we can write it as the
reward at the current time

548
00:30:31,070 --> 00:30:32,710
step plus the value
at the next time

549
00:30:32,710 --> 00:30:37,670
step minus the value at
the current time step.

550
00:30:37,670 --> 00:30:42,190
And so maybe I'll do
this in a different color

551
00:30:42,190 --> 00:30:43,170
to differentiate it.

552
00:30:43,170 --> 00:30:55,910
So now A pi of St, at can be
approximated as r, the reward,

553
00:30:55,910 --> 00:30:59,870
that you saw plus the
value at the next time

554
00:30:59,870 --> 00:31:05,350
step minus the value at
the current time step.

555
00:31:05,350 --> 00:31:12,150
And so if we then have an
estimate of V for our policy,

556
00:31:12,150 --> 00:31:14,630
then we can estimate
the advantage,

557
00:31:14,630 --> 00:31:18,790
and we can just try
to directly fit V.

558
00:31:18,790 --> 00:31:21,010
And so on the slides,
what this looks like,

559
00:31:21,010 --> 00:31:26,825
we have Q. Q can be written
as the expected future rewards

560
00:31:26,825 --> 00:31:28,450
which is the reward
at the current time

561
00:31:28,450 --> 00:31:30,110
step plus the future rewards.

562
00:31:30,110 --> 00:31:33,570
These future rewards can
be written as the value.

563
00:31:33,570 --> 00:31:36,290
And so we can think of Q as
the reward at the current time

564
00:31:36,290 --> 00:31:39,300
step plus the value
at the next time step.

565
00:31:39,300 --> 00:31:43,370

566
00:31:43,370 --> 00:31:48,610
So why is this an approximation?

567
00:31:48,610 --> 00:31:52,410
The reason for this is
that there's actually

568
00:31:52,410 --> 00:31:54,450
multiple possible next states.

569
00:31:54,450 --> 00:31:59,190
And we're just going to use one
sample from this expectation,

570
00:31:59,190 --> 00:32:01,730
which is the sample that
we saw when we rolled out

571
00:32:01,730 --> 00:32:04,010
the trajectory in our data.

572
00:32:04,010 --> 00:32:06,050
And if you want an
exact thing, you

573
00:32:06,050 --> 00:32:08,050
would need to estimate
this expectation

574
00:32:08,050 --> 00:32:10,050
with infinite samples.

575
00:32:10,050 --> 00:32:11,750
How good of an
approximation is this?

576
00:32:11,750 --> 00:32:15,630
So it is going to be a
single sample approximation.

577
00:32:15,630 --> 00:32:19,230
The good news is
that later on we'll

578
00:32:19,230 --> 00:32:22,970
see ways to use lots
of data to do this.

579
00:32:22,970 --> 00:32:27,172

580
00:32:27,172 --> 00:32:28,630
Well, so I guess
the first thing is

581
00:32:28,630 --> 00:32:30,982
that if this is
very deterministic,

582
00:32:30,982 --> 00:32:33,190
first, it's going to be a
good estimate because there

583
00:32:33,190 --> 00:32:35,293
aren't that many futures.

584
00:32:35,293 --> 00:32:36,710
If it's more
stochastic, then it's

585
00:32:36,710 --> 00:32:38,950
going to be a worse
estimate, and then we'll

586
00:32:38,950 --> 00:32:41,110
see ways in which we
can use data and take

587
00:32:41,110 --> 00:32:44,950
lots of gradient steps in a
way that makes it not too bad.

588
00:32:44,950 --> 00:32:47,510
So the question is, is
there any new interaction

589
00:32:47,510 --> 00:32:48,650
with the environment?

590
00:32:48,650 --> 00:32:53,150
So I guess we haven't yet
talked about how to fit V pi.

591
00:32:53,150 --> 00:32:56,470
So we will talk about
that in the next slide.

592
00:32:56,470 --> 00:33:00,790
So if you have some
trajectory this

593
00:33:00,790 --> 00:33:05,470
will be like the St, at and St
plus 1 from the same trajectory.

594
00:33:05,470 --> 00:33:08,470
So you need to use the
same trajectory for this.

595
00:33:08,470 --> 00:33:12,810
And sometimes I forget
to put the pi on here.

596
00:33:12,810 --> 00:33:15,170
And there is a lot of
reinforcement learning work that

597
00:33:15,170 --> 00:33:17,310
drops the pis just
for simplicity,

598
00:33:17,310 --> 00:33:19,577
but it is actually,
in some ways,

599
00:33:19,577 --> 00:33:21,410
very important to have
A pi there because it

600
00:33:21,410 --> 00:33:23,930
is estimating a value
for a particular policy,

601
00:33:23,930 --> 00:33:25,725
not for any policy.

602
00:33:25,725 --> 00:33:27,850
So the question is, why
are we choosing to estimate

603
00:33:27,850 --> 00:33:30,290
V versus Q versus A?

604
00:33:30,290 --> 00:33:33,090
It's just a little bit
simpler in practice.

605
00:33:33,090 --> 00:33:38,970
But there are also ways
to directly estimate Q.

606
00:33:38,970 --> 00:33:42,050
And actually we'll talk about
some of those ways next week

607
00:33:42,050 --> 00:33:42,590
as well.

608
00:33:42,590 --> 00:33:49,690

609
00:33:49,690 --> 00:33:51,450
So we're just going to fit V pi.

610
00:33:51,450 --> 00:33:53,630
And then once we fit V pi
we can get the advantage,

611
00:33:53,630 --> 00:33:58,450
and then plug that into our
gradient for our policy.

612
00:33:58,450 --> 00:34:01,188
We can represent V pi
as a neural network.

613
00:34:01,188 --> 00:34:02,730
And what this will
look like is we'll

614
00:34:02,730 --> 00:34:06,250
pass as input a
state and then it

615
00:34:06,250 --> 00:34:11,210
will output a
single scalar value

616
00:34:11,210 --> 00:34:14,960
that represents the value of
that state for that policy.

617
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:18,639
This neural network will
have some parameters.

618
00:34:18,639 --> 00:34:20,159
So how do we do this?

619
00:34:20,159 --> 00:34:24,980
So there's a few different
ways to estimate V pi.

620
00:34:24,980 --> 00:34:31,920

621
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:33,800
So in the very, very
initial version,

622
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:35,639
like a vanilla policy
gradient, we just

623
00:34:35,639 --> 00:34:39,080
used a single trajectory,
a single sample.

624
00:34:39,080 --> 00:34:42,520
And instead of actually
learning a model

625
00:34:42,520 --> 00:34:43,670
we just used that sample.

626
00:34:43,670 --> 00:34:46,440

627
00:34:46,440 --> 00:34:50,749
In principle, we could
also use many samples.

628
00:34:50,749 --> 00:34:56,020

629
00:34:56,020 --> 00:34:58,960
Unfortunately, if you were to
try to go to a particular state

630
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:01,040
and then take many
samples from that state,

631
00:35:01,040 --> 00:35:02,540
that means that you
would have to be

632
00:35:02,540 --> 00:35:04,720
able to reset the
world to that state

633
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:10,020
and see what happens if you
took many different rollouts

634
00:35:10,020 --> 00:35:12,100
from that same state.

635
00:35:12,100 --> 00:35:13,920
And unfortunately,
in many scenarios,

636
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:16,260
it's very hard to
reset the state.

637
00:35:16,260 --> 00:35:19,320
If you are in
simulated settings,

638
00:35:19,320 --> 00:35:21,830
sometimes you can actually
reset the simulator.

639
00:35:21,830 --> 00:35:24,080
So there is sometimes scenarios
where you can do this,

640
00:35:24,080 --> 00:35:27,530
but in practice, we
typically don't do this.

641
00:35:27,530 --> 00:35:31,540

642
00:35:31,540 --> 00:35:34,140
So since we can't
directly reset and take

643
00:35:34,140 --> 00:35:37,060
lots of samples,
what we can do is

644
00:35:37,060 --> 00:35:39,740
we can use multiple
trajectories that we had sampled

645
00:35:39,740 --> 00:35:43,660
and just train a single model
on the original single sample

646
00:35:43,660 --> 00:35:44,860
estimate.

647
00:35:44,860 --> 00:35:47,780
And because the
trajectories that we have,

648
00:35:47,780 --> 00:35:51,380
they will have some states
in them that are similar.

649
00:35:51,380 --> 00:36:00,960
You'll be able to essentially
learn how to amortize the data.

650
00:36:00,960 --> 00:36:04,180
So you'll see some examples
in the environment that

651
00:36:04,180 --> 00:36:06,700
have very similar
states and have

652
00:36:06,700 --> 00:36:11,080
corresponding future
rewards in them.

653
00:36:11,080 --> 00:36:14,160
And with that
repetition in your data,

654
00:36:14,160 --> 00:36:18,080
a neural network can have
a little bit more density

655
00:36:18,080 --> 00:36:23,000
in terms of the rewards that
you'll see for a given state.

656
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:25,960
And so what this is
going to look like is

657
00:36:25,960 --> 00:36:34,840
you can take your
data set, and you'll

658
00:36:34,840 --> 00:36:40,320
have I equals 1
to n trajectories.

659
00:36:40,320 --> 00:36:44,640
And each trajectory will
have time steps in it.

660
00:36:44,640 --> 00:36:51,160
And for all of those
trajectories and time steps,

661
00:36:51,160 --> 00:36:53,640
you'll also have
some sum of rewards

662
00:36:53,640 --> 00:36:56,640
that you observe for that.

663
00:36:56,640 --> 00:36:59,560
So you'll have
some sum of rewards

664
00:36:59,560 --> 00:37:11,910
that started at t to the end
of the rewards of the states

665
00:37:11,910 --> 00:37:13,870
and actions that you visited.

666
00:37:13,870 --> 00:37:17,150
And if you aggregate all the
trajectories in your data set,

667
00:37:17,150 --> 00:37:21,230
you can train a model
to go from the state

668
00:37:21,230 --> 00:37:25,270
that you saw to the sum of
rewards that was observed

669
00:37:25,270 --> 00:37:27,350
when you ran the policy.

670
00:37:27,350 --> 00:37:30,990
And with this, you can
form a supervised learning

671
00:37:30,990 --> 00:37:38,710
data set where this is
the label in the data set.

672
00:37:38,710 --> 00:37:40,310
And you can train
with supervised

673
00:37:40,310 --> 00:37:43,510
learning a model to take
as input this and output

674
00:37:43,510 --> 00:37:45,313
this quantity right here.

675
00:37:45,313 --> 00:37:47,230
And the number of data
points in your data set

676
00:37:47,230 --> 00:37:48,870
will be the number
of trajectories

677
00:37:48,870 --> 00:37:51,870
times the average
number of time steps.

678
00:37:51,870 --> 00:37:54,210
So the question is, each
time your policy changes,

679
00:37:54,210 --> 00:37:58,220
do you have to recollect
the data and refit this?

680
00:37:58,220 --> 00:38:00,510
With this approach, yes.

681
00:38:00,510 --> 00:38:02,630
We will see approaches later.

682
00:38:02,630 --> 00:38:05,690
This is the simplest and
dumbest approach you can

683
00:38:05,690 --> 00:38:08,930
use to fit a value function.

684
00:38:08,930 --> 00:38:12,872
And we will see approaches
where you can reuse other data.

685
00:38:12,872 --> 00:38:14,330
So the question
was, if we're going

686
00:38:14,330 --> 00:38:16,410
to be running a
lot of simulations

687
00:38:16,410 --> 00:38:19,290
or rollouts of our policy,
is it unreasonable to try

688
00:38:19,290 --> 00:38:21,570
to learn some estimate
of future states

689
00:38:21,570 --> 00:38:23,240
given the current
state and action?

690
00:38:23,240 --> 00:38:27,970

691
00:38:27,970 --> 00:38:28,990
Two things here.

692
00:38:28,990 --> 00:38:33,210
If you have a very simple state
space, a very small state space,

693
00:38:33,210 --> 00:38:36,930
you might be able
to store some things

694
00:38:36,930 --> 00:38:39,563
and form an estimate
just from storing things.

695
00:38:39,563 --> 00:38:41,730
You could also, in principle,
train a neural network

696
00:38:41,730 --> 00:38:44,450
to predict what future states
would be given current states

697
00:38:44,450 --> 00:38:46,210
and actions.

698
00:38:46,210 --> 00:38:48,953
There are a set of
approaches that do that,

699
00:38:48,953 --> 00:38:51,370
and they're called model-based
approaches and that they're

700
00:38:51,370 --> 00:38:53,930
learning a model of the world.

701
00:38:53,930 --> 00:38:56,680
And we will talk about those
approaches in a few weeks.

702
00:38:56,680 --> 00:39:00,970

703
00:39:00,970 --> 00:39:07,850
So this first approach,
run a bunch of samples,

704
00:39:07,850 --> 00:39:12,270
aggregate a data set
of our future rewards,

705
00:39:12,270 --> 00:39:16,310
and then run supervised learning
to fit an estimated value

706
00:39:16,310 --> 00:39:21,990
function where you
can form an L2 error

707
00:39:21,990 --> 00:39:27,950
between your predicted value
and the observed sum of rewards,

708
00:39:27,950 --> 00:39:29,630
and then minimize
that with respect

709
00:39:29,630 --> 00:39:32,300
to the parameters of
your value function.

710
00:39:32,300 --> 00:39:36,030

711
00:39:36,030 --> 00:39:41,550
And so what this objective
looks like is minimizing

712
00:39:41,550 --> 00:39:44,310
with respect to your
parameters the difference

713
00:39:44,310 --> 00:39:55,510
between your predicted
value and the example

714
00:39:55,510 --> 00:39:57,030
or the sum of
rewards that you saw

715
00:39:57,030 --> 00:40:01,310
in your data set for a given i.

716
00:40:01,310 --> 00:40:06,250
And you'll do this for
all of the data points

717
00:40:06,250 --> 00:40:09,010
in your data set.

718
00:40:09,010 --> 00:40:11,490
And so this actually
can also be a sum over i

719
00:40:11,490 --> 00:40:15,530
and t, just for completeness.

720
00:40:15,530 --> 00:40:17,607
So that will be the objective.

721
00:40:17,607 --> 00:40:19,690
And you can run gradient
descent on this objective

722
00:40:19,690 --> 00:40:23,870
to fit a value function
on your data point.

723
00:40:23,870 --> 00:40:26,370
Do we need to train a neural
network for every gradient step

724
00:40:26,370 --> 00:40:27,230
in this case?

725
00:40:27,230 --> 00:40:29,816
So this is actually different
from policy gradient where--

726
00:40:29,816 --> 00:40:33,290

727
00:40:33,290 --> 00:40:35,770
the gradient steps
here that we take

728
00:40:35,770 --> 00:40:37,890
are going to be trying
to fit a value function.

729
00:40:37,890 --> 00:40:41,050
And the value function
isn't changing the policy.

730
00:40:41,050 --> 00:40:43,050
And so you can actually
take many gradient steps

731
00:40:43,050 --> 00:40:47,250
on this objective to learn a
value function without having

732
00:40:47,250 --> 00:40:48,720
to recollect your data.

733
00:40:48,720 --> 00:40:51,930

734
00:40:51,930 --> 00:40:54,750
So this is the simplest approach
for training a value function.

735
00:40:54,750 --> 00:40:57,010
It's called Monte
Carlo estimation

736
00:40:57,010 --> 00:41:01,940
where essentially you're just
running lots of simulations,

737
00:41:01,940 --> 00:41:05,320
running lots of
rollouts, and then trying

738
00:41:05,320 --> 00:41:08,670
to learn how to predict the
outcome of the future rewards.

739
00:41:08,670 --> 00:41:11,440

740
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:14,920
Now, there's
actually an approach

741
00:41:14,920 --> 00:41:18,400
that's better than this.

742
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:23,320
And in particular, when we think
about these sum of rewards,

743
00:41:23,320 --> 00:41:25,620
maybe it's helpful to draw
a picture in this case.

744
00:41:25,620 --> 00:41:35,920
So we have a bunch of
trajectories that we've sampled.

745
00:41:35,920 --> 00:41:44,160
And in this case, what we're
doing is when we have a state

746
00:41:44,160 --> 00:41:51,040
right here, we are summing
the rewards here as the value.

747
00:41:51,040 --> 00:41:55,600
And as we're
learning this, we're

748
00:41:55,600 --> 00:42:01,820
starting to learn what the
value of a given state is.

749
00:42:01,820 --> 00:42:05,980
And this means
that maybe halfway

750
00:42:05,980 --> 00:42:10,460
through training when we're
regressing to future rewards--

751
00:42:10,460 --> 00:42:14,180
and if we start to get what is
a good value of this state right

752
00:42:14,180 --> 00:42:16,580
here, then we could
actually try to use

753
00:42:16,580 --> 00:42:21,660
our own estimate of this value
as the supervision target.

754
00:42:21,660 --> 00:42:25,340
And in particular,
if we know that we

755
00:42:25,340 --> 00:42:30,140
got a reward at this time step
right here to then get to here,

756
00:42:30,140 --> 00:42:35,020
then we could actually use
that reward plus our estimate

757
00:42:35,020 --> 00:42:39,740
as the supervision target
rather than just using

758
00:42:39,740 --> 00:42:43,180
the sum of future rewards.

759
00:42:43,180 --> 00:42:48,340
And so in particular,
the ideal target

760
00:42:48,340 --> 00:42:52,260
is the true sum of
expected rewards.

761
00:42:52,260 --> 00:42:55,620
This is equal to the
reward at the current time

762
00:42:55,620 --> 00:42:59,180
step plus the expected
rewards at the next time step.

763
00:42:59,180 --> 00:43:05,578

764
00:43:05,578 --> 00:43:07,120
The sum of rewards
that the next time

765
00:43:07,120 --> 00:43:09,910
step is equal to the
value of the next state.

766
00:43:09,910 --> 00:43:12,840

767
00:43:12,840 --> 00:43:15,000
And so in principle,
what we could

768
00:43:15,000 --> 00:43:19,400
do is instead of trying to
regress to future rewards,

769
00:43:19,400 --> 00:43:24,640
we could regress to the
reward at the current time

770
00:43:24,640 --> 00:43:30,480
step plus the value
at the next time step.

771
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:32,580
And so we're actually
going to be, in this case,

772
00:43:32,580 --> 00:43:36,520
using our estimate of the
value at the next time step

773
00:43:36,520 --> 00:43:38,940
as part of the
supervision target.

774
00:43:38,940 --> 00:43:44,420
So these are all for
a given trajectory,

775
00:43:44,420 --> 00:43:55,160
and this would be your new label
for your data set for a given

776
00:43:55,160 --> 00:43:57,140
state.

777
00:43:57,140 --> 00:44:00,940
So the question is, how often
do you update these V pi's?

778
00:44:00,940 --> 00:44:06,780
In practice, you can update
them every single iteration.

779
00:44:06,780 --> 00:44:08,600
If it's really expensive
to update them,

780
00:44:08,600 --> 00:44:10,642
you could update them less
frequently if you have

781
00:44:10,642 --> 00:44:12,100
a really huge neural network.

782
00:44:12,100 --> 00:44:13,980
But in practice,
typically, we update them

783
00:44:13,980 --> 00:44:17,260
every single gradient step.

784
00:44:17,260 --> 00:44:19,622
So we have these
bootstrapped estimates.

785
00:44:19,622 --> 00:44:21,580
We refer to this as
bootstrapping because we're

786
00:44:21,580 --> 00:44:24,260
actually bootstrapping off of
our own estimate of the value

787
00:44:24,260 --> 00:44:28,700
function as part of
the learning process.

788
00:44:28,700 --> 00:44:30,240
We formulate our training data.

789
00:44:30,240 --> 00:44:32,500
We're then going to use
the same exact objective

790
00:44:32,500 --> 00:44:33,960
to fit a value function.

791
00:44:33,960 --> 00:44:35,440
Just regressing
to these targets,

792
00:44:35,440 --> 00:44:39,500
we just have different
labels in our data set now.

793
00:44:39,500 --> 00:44:47,100
And these labels are changing
as we run gradient descent

794
00:44:47,100 --> 00:44:48,040
on our objective.

795
00:44:48,040 --> 00:44:50,860
And so you often will
update your labels

796
00:44:50,860 --> 00:44:53,495
every single gradient update.

797
00:44:53,495 --> 00:44:55,620
So the question is, is this
based on one trajectory

798
00:44:55,620 --> 00:44:59,210
or is this based on
multiple trajectories

799
00:44:59,210 --> 00:45:02,010
where things are close together?

800
00:45:02,010 --> 00:45:04,690
So this data set will be
over multiple trajectories

801
00:45:04,690 --> 00:45:06,930
where i is indexing
over trajectories

802
00:45:06,930 --> 00:45:11,480
and t is indexing over time
inside those trajectories.

803
00:45:11,480 --> 00:45:15,210
In terms of this label, this
will be for a given data point.

804
00:45:15,210 --> 00:45:19,450

805
00:45:19,450 --> 00:45:22,370
And you're not going to be
running multiple simulations

806
00:45:22,370 --> 00:45:24,650
from that data point,
but you're going

807
00:45:24,650 --> 00:45:28,630
to be estimating what would have
happened from your future state

808
00:45:28,630 --> 00:45:30,570
rather than a single
sample of what

809
00:45:30,570 --> 00:45:32,515
happened in that trajectory.

810
00:45:32,515 --> 00:45:33,890
And so with this,
you're actually

811
00:45:33,890 --> 00:45:36,890
able to get a better estimate
of all of the possible outcomes

812
00:45:36,890 --> 00:45:39,510
that might have happened
at the next state,

813
00:45:39,510 --> 00:45:41,810
rather than just one outcome.

814
00:45:41,810 --> 00:45:45,490
So you're asking, why is the
input to the value function

815
00:45:45,490 --> 00:45:50,490
just S and not including
previous time steps?

816
00:45:50,490 --> 00:45:50,990
Oh.

817
00:45:50,990 --> 00:45:58,510

818
00:45:58,510 --> 00:46:02,110
The ideal target for
our value function

819
00:46:02,110 --> 00:46:03,950
is the sum of future rewards.

820
00:46:03,950 --> 00:46:06,230
And you can think about
this sum of future rewards

821
00:46:06,230 --> 00:46:09,910
as the reward at the
current time step, which

822
00:46:09,910 --> 00:46:16,790
is a deterministic quantity plus
the future rewards conditioned

823
00:46:16,790 --> 00:46:18,612
on our current state.

824
00:46:18,612 --> 00:46:20,570
And so this is conditioned
on the current state

825
00:46:20,570 --> 00:46:24,870
because the future rewards is a
function of where we are right

826
00:46:24,870 --> 00:46:27,470
now, and it's actually
only a function

827
00:46:27,470 --> 00:46:30,545
of where we are right now
and not anything else.

828
00:46:30,545 --> 00:46:32,670
So the question is, does
this require that you have

829
00:46:32,670 --> 00:46:35,750
a good initial value estimate?

830
00:46:35,750 --> 00:46:37,990
Because if it's really
bad at the beginning,

831
00:46:37,990 --> 00:46:42,390
then your targets
will be really bad.

832
00:46:42,390 --> 00:46:44,930
There are some considerations
when initializing it,

833
00:46:44,930 --> 00:46:46,670
although oftentimes
you don't need

834
00:46:46,670 --> 00:46:49,130
to do anything super
fancy to initialize it,

835
00:46:49,130 --> 00:46:52,355
and you can just get started
training on this directly.

836
00:46:52,355 --> 00:46:54,730
You probably don't want to
initialize your neural network

837
00:46:54,730 --> 00:46:58,570
such that the values are like
negative 1000 or something, that

838
00:46:58,570 --> 00:46:59,670
would be bad.

839
00:46:59,670 --> 00:47:02,170
But usually we don't initialize
our neural networks that way

840
00:47:02,170 --> 00:47:02,670
anyway.

841
00:47:02,670 --> 00:47:07,022

842
00:47:07,022 --> 00:47:11,690
I don't know if I've in practice
seen any issues with this.

843
00:47:11,690 --> 00:47:14,290
But in principle, you could
also start with a Monte Carlo

844
00:47:14,290 --> 00:47:16,410
objective just to get
it in the right ballpark

845
00:47:16,410 --> 00:47:20,730
and then continue with
this update after that.

846
00:47:20,730 --> 00:47:22,430
So is this part of
backpropagation,

847
00:47:22,430 --> 00:47:24,370
and is there a better
way to than updating

848
00:47:24,370 --> 00:47:25,870
the labels every step?

849
00:47:25,870 --> 00:47:29,090
So we'll be using
backpropagation

850
00:47:29,090 --> 00:47:31,370
to optimize that
objective right there.

851
00:47:31,370 --> 00:47:34,450
You could also think about
this as propagating information

852
00:47:34,450 --> 00:47:38,890
backwards in time, which is
distinct from propagating

853
00:47:38,890 --> 00:47:42,490
gradients through the network.

854
00:47:42,490 --> 00:47:44,890
One other name for this is
called temporal difference

855
00:47:44,890 --> 00:47:47,450
learning where we're
looking at the difference

856
00:47:47,450 --> 00:47:53,536
between the future time step
and the current time step.

857
00:47:53,536 --> 00:47:56,960
But generally propagating
information through time

858
00:47:56,960 --> 00:47:59,180
is fairly distinct
from back propagation.

859
00:47:59,180 --> 00:48:01,847

860
00:48:01,847 --> 00:48:03,680
To summarize what we've
talked about so far,

861
00:48:03,680 --> 00:48:06,900
Monte Carlo were supervising
with the rollout summed rewards,

862
00:48:06,900 --> 00:48:08,920
whereas bootstrapping
were supervising

863
00:48:08,920 --> 00:48:11,800
using the reward plus
the current value

864
00:48:11,800 --> 00:48:15,040
estimate of the next state.

865
00:48:15,040 --> 00:48:18,360
And if we look at an example--

866
00:48:18,360 --> 00:48:23,840
and actually, yeah, I'll
give another exercise for you

867
00:48:23,840 --> 00:48:24,500
to figure out.

868
00:48:24,500 --> 00:48:25,667
So let's look at an example.

869
00:48:25,667 --> 00:48:27,400
So we run the policy.

870
00:48:27,400 --> 00:48:28,900
Maybe we just have
two trajectories.

871
00:48:28,900 --> 00:48:30,483
In practice we'll
have more than this.

872
00:48:30,483 --> 00:48:32,520
But say we have
two trajectories.

873
00:48:32,520 --> 00:48:36,080
One of these trajectories the
final reward was negative 1.

874
00:48:36,080 --> 00:48:39,280
The other trajectory the
final reward was one.

875
00:48:39,280 --> 00:48:42,720
And along this directory,
before the final state,

876
00:48:42,720 --> 00:48:45,800
the reward is 0.

877
00:48:45,800 --> 00:48:51,540
My question for you
is, what estimate

878
00:48:51,540 --> 00:48:55,260
would we get for two different
states for the value function?

879
00:48:55,260 --> 00:48:59,660
And in particular, if we were
training on just these two

880
00:48:59,660 --> 00:49:02,740
trajectories, and we use
Monte Carlo versus using

881
00:49:02,740 --> 00:49:05,380
temporal difference
or bootstrap learning,

882
00:49:05,380 --> 00:49:07,660
what would we estimate
the value to be

883
00:49:07,660 --> 00:49:10,120
for the blue state
for these two?

884
00:49:10,120 --> 00:49:11,620
And what would we
estimate the value

885
00:49:11,620 --> 00:49:14,980
to be for the pink
state for these two?

886
00:49:14,980 --> 00:49:17,580
So for Monte Carlo
in the blue state,

887
00:49:17,580 --> 00:49:20,580
it's going to average out
the minus 1 and the 1,

888
00:49:20,580 --> 00:49:25,300
and so this
prediction will be 0.

889
00:49:25,300 --> 00:49:28,440
Does anyone want to
share another one?

890
00:49:28,440 --> 00:49:28,940
Yeah.

891
00:49:28,940 --> 00:49:30,700
So for the Monte
Carlo for this one,

892
00:49:30,700 --> 00:49:33,540
this will be
negative 1 because it

893
00:49:33,540 --> 00:49:36,420
will be supervised to
predict the sum of rewards

894
00:49:36,420 --> 00:49:39,540
for the future for
this trajectory.

895
00:49:39,540 --> 00:49:42,780
Anyone want to share?

896
00:49:42,780 --> 00:49:44,540
Before we do that,
let's talk about--

897
00:49:44,540 --> 00:49:46,140
do you want to do this one?

898
00:49:46,140 --> 00:49:50,500
This is a little bit more
intuitive to go this way.

899
00:49:50,500 --> 00:49:53,320
Yeah, so this will also be 0.

900
00:49:53,320 --> 00:49:56,440
And then does anyone
want to do the back part?

901
00:49:56,440 --> 00:50:00,260
Yeah, so for this one, it'll
see this blue one in the future.

902
00:50:00,260 --> 00:50:03,000
This will have a 0,
and then this will also

903
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:06,280
be assigned the hat of 0.

904
00:50:06,280 --> 00:50:08,280
And so this is an
example of where

905
00:50:08,280 --> 00:50:11,360
Monte Carlo and the
bootstrap estimate

906
00:50:11,360 --> 00:50:12,880
are going to be different.

907
00:50:12,880 --> 00:50:16,240
And arguably, we get a
more accurate estimate

908
00:50:16,240 --> 00:50:19,320
from the bootstrap
value because it's

909
00:50:19,320 --> 00:50:22,720
able to look at its
estimate of the value

910
00:50:22,720 --> 00:50:25,480
and actually aggregate
information across trajectories

911
00:50:25,480 --> 00:50:28,320
more effectively.

912
00:50:28,320 --> 00:50:31,440
Why the Monte Carlo is
minus 1 by the top right?

913
00:50:31,440 --> 00:50:34,700
So the value for this one
is going to be minus 1

914
00:50:34,700 --> 00:50:37,000
because the Monte Carlo
objective is supervising

915
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:39,120
to the sum of future
rewards that are

916
00:50:39,120 --> 00:50:40,660
observed for each trajectory.

917
00:50:40,660 --> 00:50:43,020
And so for this
trajectory, which is black,

918
00:50:43,020 --> 00:50:47,840
it only sees the sum of future
rewards along this trajectory.

919
00:50:47,840 --> 00:50:49,620
So it only sees negative 1.

920
00:50:49,620 --> 00:50:53,660
And anything over here, along
this trajectory for Monte Carlo,

921
00:50:53,660 --> 00:50:56,060
would have a target
of one because it only

922
00:50:56,060 --> 00:50:59,100
sees the future
trajectories here

923
00:50:59,100 --> 00:51:02,220
because it doesn't bootstrap
off of its estimate that was

924
00:51:02,220 --> 00:51:04,420
using all of the trajectories.

925
00:51:04,420 --> 00:51:07,620
So the Monte Carlo blue
state, it's going to be--

926
00:51:07,620 --> 00:51:09,420
that exact state
is in the buffer

927
00:51:09,420 --> 00:51:14,240
or is in your data set
for both trajectories.

928
00:51:14,240 --> 00:51:17,940
And so it's going to
average the reward for them.

929
00:51:17,940 --> 00:51:19,500
But this is like
the only scenario

930
00:51:19,500 --> 00:51:23,675
where Monte Carlo is
going to get information

931
00:51:23,675 --> 00:51:25,300
from both trajectories
because you have

932
00:51:25,300 --> 00:51:27,580
the same exact state
in your data set

933
00:51:27,580 --> 00:51:29,700
with two different labels.

934
00:51:29,700 --> 00:51:32,820
Why is the bootstrap
blue state 0?

935
00:51:32,820 --> 00:51:36,000
So this is also going to be
averaging the future rewards.

936
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:38,220
It doesn't have anything
necessarily special

937
00:51:38,220 --> 00:51:40,020
to bootstrap off of.

938
00:51:40,020 --> 00:51:44,300
And so for the same reason
that Monte Carlo is 0,

939
00:51:44,300 --> 00:51:46,770
this will also be V0.

940
00:51:46,770 --> 00:51:49,250
So basically everything
after the blue state

941
00:51:49,250 --> 00:51:52,110
will have a value estimate
of negative 1 here.

942
00:51:52,110 --> 00:51:55,490
And everything after the
blue state on the block

943
00:51:55,490 --> 00:52:00,210
trajectory is going to have
an estimate of negative ones.

944
00:52:00,210 --> 00:52:03,730
We should move on.

945
00:52:03,730 --> 00:52:09,850
So one question you might
ask looking at this is--

946
00:52:09,850 --> 00:52:11,590
there might also
be a middle ground.

947
00:52:11,590 --> 00:52:16,290
So Monte Carlo is going
to have high variance,

948
00:52:16,290 --> 00:52:18,530
but it is completely unbiased.

949
00:52:18,530 --> 00:52:22,570
Whereas the bootstrap estimate
has much lower variance,

950
00:52:22,570 --> 00:52:25,610
but it actually can be incorrect
because your current estimate

951
00:52:25,610 --> 00:52:28,170
of the value function is
going to have inevitably

952
00:52:28,170 --> 00:52:31,450
at least a small
amount of error in it.

953
00:52:31,450 --> 00:52:38,050
And so there might be a way to
potentially balance this bias

954
00:52:38,050 --> 00:52:41,370
and variance a bit more.

955
00:52:41,370 --> 00:52:43,730
And one way that we
can think about this

956
00:52:43,730 --> 00:52:46,430
is if we have our
trajectories, we

957
00:52:46,430 --> 00:52:47,990
can think of the
Monte Carlo version

958
00:52:47,990 --> 00:52:50,830
as using the sum
of rewards averaged

959
00:52:50,830 --> 00:52:54,270
over those trajectories,
whereas the Tt version

960
00:52:54,270 --> 00:52:57,230
is going to be looking
at the first reward

961
00:52:57,230 --> 00:52:59,810
plus the value right after that.

962
00:52:59,810 --> 00:53:04,350
So it's r plus V for just like a
temporal difference of just one

963
00:53:04,350 --> 00:53:06,750
time step.

964
00:53:06,750 --> 00:53:10,330
And you can actually interpolate
between these two estimates.

965
00:53:10,330 --> 00:53:12,730
And instead of using
just one time step,

966
00:53:12,730 --> 00:53:14,370
you could use n time steps.

967
00:53:14,370 --> 00:53:18,950
So you could look at sum
of the rewards for n time

968
00:53:18,950 --> 00:53:22,390
steps in the future before
the variance has increased

969
00:53:22,390 --> 00:53:26,630
substantially, and then use your
value estimate after those n

970
00:53:26,630 --> 00:53:28,830
time steps.

971
00:53:28,830 --> 00:53:32,830
And what this would look like
is an equation like this,

972
00:53:32,830 --> 00:53:36,790
where instead of just using
one reward right here,

973
00:53:36,790 --> 00:53:42,970
and instead of using all
of the sum of rewards,

974
00:53:42,970 --> 00:53:48,170
you can sum over the
next n minus 1 rewards,

975
00:53:48,170 --> 00:53:52,410
and then use your value
estimate at t plus n.

976
00:53:52,410 --> 00:53:59,370
And so this looks like
a sum over from t to t

977
00:53:59,370 --> 00:54:10,450
plus n minus 1 r of S, a, plus
your estimate of the value

978
00:54:10,450 --> 00:54:13,590
at t plus n.

979
00:54:13,590 --> 00:54:17,792

980
00:54:17,792 --> 00:54:19,250
In this case, this
is going to have

981
00:54:19,250 --> 00:54:22,650
a lot less variance than the
full Monte Carlo, especially

982
00:54:22,650 --> 00:54:25,650
if you have smaller n.

983
00:54:25,650 --> 00:54:28,210
And it's going to
be potentially less

984
00:54:28,210 --> 00:54:31,370
biased than just using a
one step temporal difference

985
00:54:31,370 --> 00:54:33,450
bootstrap.

986
00:54:33,450 --> 00:54:37,090
And so essentially you're
going to have more information

987
00:54:37,090 --> 00:54:42,600
about the rewards that
were directly observed.

988
00:54:42,600 --> 00:54:44,420
So this can be written as this.

989
00:54:44,420 --> 00:54:48,120
And in this case, this
now becomes your target

990
00:54:48,120 --> 00:54:51,160
for supervised learning.

991
00:54:51,160 --> 00:54:54,800
And yeah, this is often
called n step returns

992
00:54:54,800 --> 00:54:58,360
because you're summing
over n time steps.

993
00:54:58,360 --> 00:55:05,320
In practice, the bootstrap
version, if you set n equals t,

994
00:55:05,320 --> 00:55:07,560
this is full Monte Carlo.

995
00:55:07,560 --> 00:55:10,666
Oftentimes somewhere
in between 1 and nt

996
00:55:10,666 --> 00:55:12,760
works the best in practice.

997
00:55:12,760 --> 00:55:15,440
And this can be especially
useful in scenarios

998
00:55:15,440 --> 00:55:17,440
where you have very
small time steps

999
00:55:17,440 --> 00:55:19,240
because if your time
step is, I don't know,

1000
00:55:19,240 --> 00:55:20,680
10 milliseconds
or something like

1001
00:55:20,680 --> 00:55:26,920
that, then very little will have
happened in between those time

1002
00:55:26,920 --> 00:55:28,120
steps.

1003
00:55:28,120 --> 00:55:30,820
If you have a very
long time trajectory,

1004
00:55:30,820 --> 00:55:34,090
can you use both of
these, you're asking?

1005
00:55:34,090 --> 00:55:36,840

1006
00:55:36,840 --> 00:55:37,620
Oh, yeah.

1007
00:55:37,620 --> 00:55:44,300

1008
00:55:44,300 --> 00:55:48,300
You could add all of these to
your data set in principle.

1009
00:55:48,300 --> 00:55:55,020
I think that typically the
high variance like labels,

1010
00:55:55,020 --> 00:55:56,420
the things that
are high variance

1011
00:55:56,420 --> 00:56:02,000
can deteriorate learning because
it leads to more stochasticity.

1012
00:56:02,000 --> 00:56:05,960
And likewise, if your
values are not very good,

1013
00:56:05,960 --> 00:56:08,523
then this can also
be a challenge.

1014
00:56:08,523 --> 00:56:09,940
And so in practice,
you'll usually

1015
00:56:09,940 --> 00:56:14,660
just pick one of these versions
and supervise with that.

1016
00:56:14,660 --> 00:56:18,160
In principle, you
could combine them.

1017
00:56:18,160 --> 00:56:20,320
But yeah, in practice
usually you don't.

1018
00:56:20,320 --> 00:56:23,110

1019
00:56:23,110 --> 00:56:24,960
One aside here.

1020
00:56:24,960 --> 00:56:28,160
So far we've been
summing rewards.

1021
00:56:28,160 --> 00:56:31,340
And we actually talked
in the first lecture

1022
00:56:31,340 --> 00:56:34,460
about how, in some
cases, we might not

1023
00:56:34,460 --> 00:56:37,320
want to just blindly sum
all rewards in the future.

1024
00:56:37,320 --> 00:56:39,800
And in particular, if
you think about scenarios

1025
00:56:39,800 --> 00:56:44,780
where the length of the
episode is infinite or very,

1026
00:56:44,780 --> 00:56:49,240
very long, then your
estimate of the value

1027
00:56:49,240 --> 00:56:53,000
can get very large
in those cases,

1028
00:56:53,000 --> 00:56:55,080
and so you might
not want to just

1029
00:56:55,080 --> 00:56:58,840
be summing over all of
the rewards in the future.

1030
00:56:58,840 --> 00:57:02,198
And so a simple trick
to handle the fact

1031
00:57:02,198 --> 00:57:04,240
that your estimates of
the value can get very big

1032
00:57:04,240 --> 00:57:10,400
is to weight your objective,
weight the sum of rewards

1033
00:57:10,400 --> 00:57:14,280
to be biased towards
the earlier rewards.

1034
00:57:14,280 --> 00:57:16,960
And the way that
you can write this

1035
00:57:16,960 --> 00:57:24,160
is, instead of having r plus V,
you can add a coefficient right

1036
00:57:24,160 --> 00:57:25,820
here.

1037
00:57:25,820 --> 00:57:28,560
If this coefficient is 1,
it's the same as before.

1038
00:57:28,560 --> 00:57:32,140
But you can set it to
something like 0.9 or 0.99.

1039
00:57:32,140 --> 00:57:33,640
And what this is
going to do is it's

1040
00:57:33,640 --> 00:57:36,080
going to say that I care
more about the rewards

1041
00:57:36,080 --> 00:57:39,540
at the current time step
and less about the rewards

1042
00:57:39,540 --> 00:57:40,890
at the future time steps.

1043
00:57:40,890 --> 00:57:47,100

1044
00:57:47,100 --> 00:57:50,100
Adding this discount
factor that's

1045
00:57:50,100 --> 00:57:52,180
discounting future
rewards is actually

1046
00:57:52,180 --> 00:57:56,660
can be viewed as changing the
decision process slightly.

1047
00:57:56,660 --> 00:57:58,740
So it's actually
equivalent to saying

1048
00:57:58,740 --> 00:58:04,940
that there is some probability
that the agent will

1049
00:58:04,940 --> 00:58:07,460
die at any given point in time.

1050
00:58:07,460 --> 00:58:09,180
And so this is
equivalent to saying

1051
00:58:09,180 --> 00:58:13,660
that with some
probability-- so say

1052
00:58:13,660 --> 00:58:21,140
gamma is like 0.99, in
this case, using this form

1053
00:58:21,140 --> 00:58:23,060
is equivalent to
saying that there's

1054
00:58:23,060 --> 00:58:26,660
a 1% chance that the agent will
enter some state that has 0

1055
00:58:26,660 --> 00:58:28,650
reward and stay there forever.

1056
00:58:28,650 --> 00:58:31,620

1057
00:58:31,620 --> 00:58:35,540
And so if you have some
original MDP right here,

1058
00:58:35,540 --> 00:58:38,610
the way that this discount
factor would change

1059
00:58:38,610 --> 00:58:45,210
the MDP is it would say that
with probability 1 minus gamma,

1060
00:58:45,210 --> 00:58:50,330
you enter this death
state where you can't

1061
00:58:50,330 --> 00:58:52,370
do anything else after that.

1062
00:58:52,370 --> 00:58:57,570
And the original probabilities
in the rest of the MDP

1063
00:58:57,570 --> 00:59:01,557
are all multiplied by gamma.

1064
00:59:01,557 --> 00:59:03,890
The question is, is there any
relationship between gamma

1065
00:59:03,890 --> 00:59:05,850
and these n step things?

1066
00:59:05,850 --> 00:59:08,950
If gamma is small, would
that change like what you do?

1067
00:59:08,950 --> 00:59:12,297

1068
00:59:12,297 --> 00:59:14,130
The way that you would
modify this objective

1069
00:59:14,130 --> 00:59:15,410
would be gamma to the n.

1070
00:59:15,410 --> 00:59:19,930

1071
00:59:19,930 --> 00:59:22,003
Possibly, I mean, I
think that they're all

1072
00:59:22,003 --> 00:59:24,170
interconnected in the sense
that the choice of gamma

1073
00:59:24,170 --> 00:59:26,310
might depend on the environment.

1074
00:59:26,310 --> 00:59:30,513
And, generally, if going to be
looking further into the future,

1075
00:59:30,513 --> 00:59:32,930
that's because you're going
to be in an environment that's

1076
00:59:32,930 --> 00:59:35,130
more deterministic or
a smaller time steps,

1077
00:59:35,130 --> 00:59:38,230
and that would also affect
the choice of gamma.

1078
00:59:38,230 --> 00:59:41,427
The question was, in terms of
choosing how far you look ahead,

1079
00:59:41,427 --> 00:59:43,510
does that affect whether
you're prioritizing short

1080
00:59:43,510 --> 00:59:45,670
versus long term?

1081
00:59:45,670 --> 00:59:49,750
In this case, it is
more a factor of--

1082
00:59:49,750 --> 00:59:52,950
so it doesn't actually
affect how greedy

1083
00:59:52,950 --> 00:59:56,070
you are because the value
function is representing

1084
00:59:56,070 --> 00:59:57,130
the sum of rewards.

1085
00:59:57,130 --> 00:59:59,950
And so these are
still estimating

1086
00:59:59,950 --> 01:00:02,390
the same exact quantity.

1087
01:00:02,390 --> 01:00:05,790
It's more a factor of
how far in the future

1088
01:00:05,790 --> 01:00:07,930
are you looking ahead
in terms of the rewards.

1089
01:00:07,930 --> 01:00:11,570
And it's more a factor of how
much is the variance increasing.

1090
01:00:11,570 --> 01:00:15,170
And if the variance
increases somewhat slowly,

1091
01:00:15,170 --> 01:00:17,830
then you can look further
ahead into the future.

1092
01:00:17,830 --> 01:00:19,990
If it increases very,
very quickly, then you

1093
01:00:19,990 --> 01:00:22,050
might want to use a smaller n.

1094
01:00:22,050 --> 01:00:25,655

1095
01:00:25,655 --> 01:00:27,530
So let's walk through
the full algorithm now.

1096
01:00:27,530 --> 01:00:31,310
So we talked about estimating
value functions, now let's

1097
01:00:31,310 --> 01:00:33,130
actually put this into practice.

1098
01:00:33,130 --> 01:00:35,730
So what we can do is we
can sample a batch of data

1099
01:00:35,730 --> 01:00:40,170
from our policy, then we
will fit our value estimate

1100
01:00:40,170 --> 01:00:42,850
to the summed
rewards in the data.

1101
01:00:42,850 --> 01:00:48,330
And we can do this by looking at
the rewards for a short period

1102
01:00:48,330 --> 01:00:50,050
of time and then the value.

1103
01:00:50,050 --> 01:00:53,130
So this would be the
equation for n step returns.

1104
01:00:53,130 --> 01:00:55,890
And then when we fit this
we'll run multiple gradient

1105
01:00:55,890 --> 01:00:58,690
steps on this objective
that's trying to minimize

1106
01:00:58,690 --> 01:01:00,370
the difference
between our estimates

1107
01:01:00,370 --> 01:01:02,120
and the observed future rewards.

1108
01:01:02,120 --> 01:01:04,770

1109
01:01:04,770 --> 01:01:07,070
Then once we have our
estimate of the value,

1110
01:01:07,070 --> 01:01:09,890
then we can use that to
estimate the advantage

1111
01:01:09,890 --> 01:01:11,850
of a particular
action by looking

1112
01:01:11,850 --> 01:01:14,690
at the reward plus the
value at the next time

1113
01:01:14,690 --> 01:01:17,470
step minus the value at
the current time step.

1114
01:01:17,470 --> 01:01:20,490
So this is telling us
according to our value function

1115
01:01:20,490 --> 01:01:24,410
and the reward we saw, how
good is the action that

1116
01:01:24,410 --> 01:01:27,210
was taken in the data set.

1117
01:01:27,210 --> 01:01:30,490
And then we can use this to
get a gradient for our policy

1118
01:01:30,490 --> 01:01:34,470
where we will be increasing
the likelihood of actions that

1119
01:01:34,470 --> 01:01:36,530
are estimated to
be advantageous,

1120
01:01:36,530 --> 01:01:38,310
and decreasing the
likelihood for actions

1121
01:01:38,310 --> 01:01:41,310
that are not advantageous.

1122
01:01:41,310 --> 01:01:43,950
And then lastly, we
update our policy

1123
01:01:43,950 --> 01:01:48,652
by applying this gradient
step to our policy parameters,

1124
01:01:48,652 --> 01:01:50,110
and then we can
repeat this process

1125
01:01:50,110 --> 01:01:52,760
to then sample a new batch
of data for our policy.

1126
01:01:52,760 --> 01:01:55,390

1127
01:01:55,390 --> 01:01:57,450
Now, note here we have
two neural networks.

1128
01:01:57,450 --> 01:02:00,150
One is for the policy and one
is for the value function.

1129
01:02:00,150 --> 01:02:01,830
This is called an
actor-critic algorithm

1130
01:02:01,830 --> 01:02:04,870
because one of the networks is
the actor, one of the networks

1131
01:02:04,870 --> 01:02:06,750
is the value function,
which can also

1132
01:02:06,750 --> 01:02:10,030
be called a critic
because it's criticizing

1133
01:02:10,030 --> 01:02:16,950
how good a state and policy
is, and that's our algorithm.

1134
01:02:16,950 --> 01:02:18,990
And coming back to
the examples that we

1135
01:02:18,990 --> 01:02:20,630
saw before, this
is something that's

1136
01:02:20,630 --> 01:02:22,072
able to actually
make use of data,

1137
01:02:22,072 --> 01:02:24,030
for example, that takes
a step forward and then

1138
01:02:24,030 --> 01:02:27,610
falls backwards because you can
actually learn from the data,

1139
01:02:27,610 --> 01:02:29,670
especially when you use
bootstrapped estimates,

1140
01:02:29,670 --> 01:02:33,500
that taking a step forward
is advantageous and has

1141
01:02:33,500 --> 01:02:36,540
a positive advantage
compared to other actions

1142
01:02:36,540 --> 01:02:37,450
that you might take.

1143
01:02:37,450 --> 01:02:39,700
And this is going to be
making much more efficient use

1144
01:02:39,700 --> 01:02:41,800
of the data that
we've collected.

1145
01:02:41,800 --> 01:02:45,432

1146
01:02:45,432 --> 01:02:46,140
So review so far.

1147
01:02:46,140 --> 01:02:47,580
We've talked about
policy gradients, which

1148
01:02:47,580 --> 01:02:50,122
is trying to do more of the good
stuff, less of the bad stuff

1149
01:02:50,122 --> 01:02:51,460
based on what you observe.

1150
01:02:51,460 --> 01:02:54,438
Actor-critic are learning to
estimate what's good and bad,

1151
01:02:54,438 --> 01:02:55,980
and then doing more
of the good stuff

1152
01:02:55,980 --> 01:02:59,220
according to your estimate.

1153
01:02:59,220 --> 01:03:02,900
And we can get a better estimate
by using a neural network

1154
01:03:02,900 --> 01:03:06,660
to try to explicitly
estimate the value function.

1155
01:03:06,660 --> 01:03:08,580
And then we introduced
this third step

1156
01:03:08,580 --> 01:03:11,540
where we are fitting this model
as part of the improvement

1157
01:03:11,540 --> 01:03:13,200
process.

1158
01:03:13,200 --> 01:03:14,700
We talked about
three different ways

1159
01:03:14,700 --> 01:03:16,580
to estimate value,
either directly looking

1160
01:03:16,580 --> 01:03:19,800
at some rewards, bootstrapping
off the value function,

1161
01:03:19,800 --> 01:03:21,380
and this hybrid.

1162
01:03:21,380 --> 01:03:25,770
And this is often referred
to as policy evaluation.

1163
01:03:25,770 --> 01:03:30,000

